# Paper 3 — Canonical Terminology

A reference for keeping wording consistent. Each row: **canonical term** | what it means | deprecated / do-not-use.

## 1. The two analytical tools (the contribution, C2)

| Canonical | Meaning | Do NOT use |
|---|---|---|
| **Bound-and-Gap decomposition** (Tool 1) | `GAP = H_up + M_Φ`; diagnostic split of the value of wave composition | "M4", "the framework" (loosely), "Bound-and-Gap framework" loosely as if it predicts |
| **Model-Dominance Hedge Rule** (Tool 2) | closed-form robust wave-structure selection under elevator-model uncertainty | "M5", "the hedge", "dispatch rule" if it implies operational dispatch |

- Refer to them collectively as **"two analytical tools"** (the contribution), never as "two stages" (those belong to the problem; see §5).
- Tool roles, when contrasting: Bound-and-Gap is **diagnostic** ("measures what a wave-structure choice is worth and how much is reachable"); Hedge Rule is **prescriptive** ("selects the robust one").

## 2. Elevator models

| Canonical | Meaning | Do NOT use |
|---|---|---|
| **M1 / throughput abstraction** (a.k.a. throughput aggregation) | `n_elevators × capacity` independent single-rider servers | "abstraction" alone when ambiguous |
| **M2 / true co-occupancy batching** | `n_elevators` servers, each batches up to `capacity` matching `(src,dst)` requests | "batched" alone when ambiguous |
| **M3 / stochastic batching** | M2 plus lognormal per-phase noise (`stochastic_sigma > 0`) | "noisy model" |

- The family is written **{M1, M2, M3}**; in the Abstract unpack it in words ("a throughput abstraction, true co-occupancy batching, and a stochastic variant").

## 3. The structured representation Φ

| Canonical | Meaning | Do NOT use |
|---|---|---|
| **Φ = (C, I, T)** | three-dimensional representation of wave composition | "fingerprint" (ok informally), "surrogate" |
| **C = vertical spread** | Shannon entropy over the floor distribution; **HIGH C = activity SPREAD across many floors** | ⚠ "vertical concentration" (REVERSED MEANING — recurring hazard, do not use) |
| **I = directional imbalance** | up-vs-down asymmetry | |
| **T = temporal clustering** | CV of per-order release times (0 unless waves are staggered) | |

- Φ is a **conceptual / structured decomposition**, NOT a **predictive surrogate**. The "Φ predicts makespan" claim was tested and **retired**: never reintroduce predicts/estimates/forecasts language for Φ. Use **selects / recovers / captures / represents**.

## 4. Decomposition components

| Canonical | Meaning | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| **GAP** | value of wave structure = `H_up + M_Φ` (= UB − LB) | "value of wave composition/design" |
| **H_up** = structural ceiling | partition-intrinsic upper-tail headroom; **capacity-side**, unrecoverable by any Φ-policy | ~72% of mean GAP (capacity-side dominant) |
| **M_Φ** = policy-recoverable slack | the part a better Φ-informed policy can recover; equals the **SPO regret** of the Φ-induced partition-constant predictor (Theorem 1) | ~28% of mean GAP |

- **"regret" wording rule:** in the **§4 formal statement** keep the technical term **"SPO regret"** (Theorem 1 = the literature bridge). In the **Abstract / plain prose**, prefer **"decision loss of predict-then-optimize"** / 中文 **"决策损失"**; the author finds bare "regret / 决策遗憾" too abstract.
- Headline managerial reading: **"capacity-bound > policy-bound"** (H_up dominates), i.e. "the binding limitation on makespan is predominantly capacity-side".

## 5. Stage (problem) vs tool (contribution) — keep distinct

| Canonical | Belongs to | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| **tactical stage** | the PROBLEM (two-stage architecture) | wave composition: which orders to release together |
| **operational stage** | the PROBLEM | AMRs deliver the released orders through the shared elevators (held FIXED in this paper) |
| **two analytical tools** | the CONTRIBUTION | both operate on the **tactical** stage |

- Do not let "two stages" and "two tools" collide. Canonical bridge sentence pattern: *"We hold the operational stage fixed and study the tactical stage with two analytical tools."*
- **wave composition** = the tactical decision. **corner** = a region of the 2×2 (C, I) partition. **wave-structure** = a corner / type of wave.
- **"policy" is double-edged, always disambiguate.** It can mean the *tactical* wave-composition/release rule (the P0-P7 selection rules, the Φ-policy, the Hedge Rule's output) or the *operational* AMR dispatch rule (`fifo`/`cluster`, held FIXED). In prose call the tactical one a **wave-release policy** / 中文 **放行策略**, and the operational one the **dispatch rule** / **operational dispatch** / 中文 **操作阶段调度**. NEVER call the Hedge Rule a "dispatch policy" (it releases waves, it does not dispatch AMRs). Bare "policy" / "策略" is acceptable only when an adjacent qualifier already pins the layer ("policy on Φ", "policy component M_Φ", "seven-policy contest", "policy-recoverable", "Φ-informed policy"). Avoid "operating policy" (reads as operational).

## 6. Chain dominance / Proposition 2

| Canonical | Meaning |
|---|---|
| **chain dominance** | per wave, `C_max(W; M1) ≤ C_max(W; M2)` (throughput abstraction is the optimistic/faster model) |
| **Proposition 2** | the conditional sufficient-condition theorem establishing chain dominance (common dispatch order + no batch-overtaking) |
| **batch-overtaking** | the violation mechanism: a synchronized M2 batch finishes a group before M1's staggered slots; the ~0.8% violations |

- In the **Abstract**, refer to it descriptively ("a conditional dominance result we establish"), **not by number** ("Proposition 2" reads abrupt in a standalone abstract). Use the number in §4 / Appendix.
- It is **conditional**, not unconditional; state this as a strength. Grounded in: stochastic comparison/coupling, server-count monotonicity, batch-service regime-dependence (see `references_chain_dominance.bib`).
- ⚠ The "individual-rider model is optimistic" sign is the **mirror image** of classic elevator-RTT results; always state the two setup assumptions (same-`(src,dst)` batching; `n·c` vs `n` server-count asymmetry) when claiming it.

## 7. Contribution structure and verdicts (Option B / path A)

- **C1** problem formulation; **C2** methodology (the two tools, three results: Prop 1, Theorem 1, Theorem 2, plus Prop 2 / Cor 1 / Cor 2); **C3** empirical insights (two managerial outcomes, framed as the **capacity-vs-policy diagnosis**, not as a baseline horse-race).
- Verdicts (report honestly, never re-judge): **H-D1 PARTIAL** (Bound-and-Gap; signal-resolution gate 57/72 = 79.2%, one short of 58/72 = 80%); **H-D2 PASS** (Hedge Rule). Frame 79.2% as **discriminating power / power-limited specificity** (the 15 unresolved cells are the low-GAP ones; AUC 0.92), not as weakness.

